Apple plans to bring <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-intelligence/">Apple Intelligence</a> to China in iOS 27.3 after two years of regulatory delays, reports <em><a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-14/apple-prepares-to-debut-first-ai-features-in-china-in-december">Bloomberg</a></em>. The launch will include basic &zwnj;Apple Intelligence&zwnj; features for things like photo editing, but <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/siri/">Siri</a> AI won't be available. <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/image-playground/">Image Playground</a> and the newer version of <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/genmoji/">Genmoji</a> will be missing, plus text generation tools will be limited.<br/>
<br/>
<img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-963902" src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2024/09/apple-intelligence-black.jpeg" alt="" width="1400" height="788" /><br/>
The <em><a href="https://www.scmp.com/tech/big-tech/article/3370893/china-approves-alibaba-ai-models-use-iphone-duo-and-other-apple-handsets">South China Morning Post</a></em> today reported that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently gave Apple network access permits to add Alibaba's Qwen and Tongyi Wanxiang models to the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-duo/">iPhone Duo</a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-18-pro/">iPhone 18 Pro</a>, and earlier iPhones, paving the way for an &zwnj;Apple Intelligence&zwnj; debut.<br/>
<br/>
Apple introduced the first &zwnj;Apple Intelligence&zwnj; features in iOS 18.1, but they have been missing from China since launch. Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/07/15/apple-intelligence-cleared-to-launch-in-china/">received initial clearance</a> to launch &zwnj;Apple Intelligence&zwnj; in China in July, but needed additional clearance for a consumer launch.<br/>
<br/>
China restricts foreign AI technology, which meant Apple had to partner with Alibaba and Baidu to launch &zwnj;Apple Intelligence&zwnj; in the country. Apple also has to allow the Chinese government to approve ongoing updates to the AI models.<br/>
<br/>
Competing smartphone makers in China like Huawei have long offered artificial intelligence features, and Apple has been at a disadvantage without comparable tools.<br/>
<br/>
